<article>
<p class="lede">Anyone can type “create a will for me” into a chatbot and get a document in seconds. It may use legal terms, look organized, and seem personal. In estate planning, looking professional is not enough.</p>
<p>We often meet well-intentioned Michigan families who learn too late that a convenient DIY option led to real consequences. Estate planning takes legal guidance, careful drafting, state-specific analysis, and human judgment.</p>

<div class="take"><b>The short answer</b><p>An AI-generated plan can look authoritative while hiding omissions that a non-lawyer will not spot. Those errors often surface only after you have died or become incapacitated, when your family is left to sort it out in Michigan probate court.</p></div>

<h2 id="state">AI can misapply state-specific law</h2>
<p>Estate planning rules differ from state to state, and the differences decide whether a will, trust, or healthcare proxy is valid. In Michigan, specific rules govern:</p>
<ul class="check">
<li>How many witnesses a will needs</li>
<li>How marital property and surviving spouse rights are treated</li>
<li>Lady Bird deeds, which can pass real estate to named beneficiaries outside probate</li>
<li>How revocable trusts are funded and administered under the Michigan Trust Code</li>
</ul>
<p>A tool trained on general internet data can blend rules from different states and produce a document that may not hold up here. A Michigan attorney knows the written law and how probate courts in places like Oakland, Wayne, and Macomb counties apply it in practice.</p>

<h2 id="signing">A signing mistake can undo a good plan</h2>
<p>Even a well-drafted document can fail if it is not signed and witnessed correctly. Michigan has specific execution requirements, and a mistake with witnesses or notarization can create doubt about whether your documents are valid.</p>
<div class="vs"><div><span>An AI tool</span><p>Generates text. It cannot supervise the signing, confirm witnesses qualify, or document that you signed freely.</p></div><div><span>An attorney</span><p>Runs the signing, checks every requirement, and records the process to head off later court challenges.</p></div></div>

<h2 id="questions">AI cannot ask the tough questions</h2>
<p>A plan has to work inside a real family, with real relationships, history, and conflict. An attorney asks what you may not know to ask yourself:</p>
<ul class="check ask">
<li>Why this child as backup trustee, and will your other children accept that choice?</li>
<li>Has anyone pressured you to make this change?</li>
<li>Does a beneficiary struggle with money, substance abuse, creditors, or an unstable marriage? Would an outright inheritance create more risk than protection?</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>AI answers the exact prompt you type. It cannot notice hesitation, tension, or a detail that does not add up.</blockquote>

<h2 id="decisions">Sensitive decisions need human counsel</h2>
<p>Some of the most personal choices in a plan are the ones where judgment matters most:</p>
<ul class="check">
<li>Dividing assets unequally among children</li>
<li>Protecting a beneficiary with special needs or substance abuse issues</li>
<li>Balancing a surviving spouse’s needs against children from a prior marriage</li>
</ul>
<p>We help you think through the practical and emotional results of each choice, and we suggest ways to reduce future conflict, such as family meetings, letters of explanation, or protective trusts. An attorney can also recognize when a client may be acting under pressure from someone else.</p>

<h2 id="privacy">Privacy and legal privilege</h2>
<p>When you enter private family and financial details into a public AI tool, that exchange is generally not protected by attorney-client privilege, and depending on the tool’s terms it may be retained or used to improve the system. Conversations with your attorney are covered by professional duties of confidentiality.</p>

<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<details><summary>Can AI coordinate my plan with my actual assets?</summary><p>Generally no. AI tools focus on producing documents. They cannot inspect your deeds, confirm how your accounts are titled, or review life insurance and retirement beneficiary designations. If your assets are not coordinated with your documents, the plan may not work as intended.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if I already used AI to draft a will or trust?</summary><p>Have a qualified attorney review it as soon as possible. We can look for missing Michigan-specific provisions, confirm it was signed correctly, and check that it does what you want.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I use AI as a starting point?</summary><p>Yes, to brainstorm what you want or to organize a list of assets and questions. Treat it as a conversation starter with an attorney, not a finished product. The real planning happens in consultation.</p></details>

<div class="cta" id="talk">
<h2>Plan with someone who will be accountable for it</h2>
<p>The real test of an estate plan comes when you are no longer here to explain what you meant. Talk with a Michigan estate planning attorney who can build a plan that works when your family needs it.</p>
